<!doctype html><html lang="en-CA"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>Home | PictureThis Production</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:wght@400;600&family=Archivo:wght@400;600&display=swap"/><style is:global>:root{--color-ground:#FAF7F2;--color-surface:#FFFFFF;--color-elevated:#F1E8D8;--color-ink:#221A12;--color-muted:#6F6050;--color-faint:#8A7B68;--color-accent:#D98E1E;--color-accent-soft:#FDEBC8;--color-on-accent:#1C1305;--color-line:#E2D6C2;--color-civic:#203DA5;--color-civic-soft:#EEF2FC;--color-civic-ink:#14265B;--color-civic-muted:#536185;--color-civic-line:#CCD5ED;--radius-card:18px;--radius-pill:999px;--radius-ngo:4px;--spacing-page:72px;--spacing-section:88px;--spacing-block:32px;--spacing-item:24px;--spacing-tight:16px;--font-display:Fraunces, Georgia, serif;--font-body:Archivo, Helvetica, sans-serif;--font-ngo:DM Sans, sans-serif;--font-civic:Space Grotesk, sans-serif;--text-hero:64px;--text-h1:52px;--text-h2:32px;--text-h3:20px;--text-body:16px;--text-small:13px;--text-poster:88px;--text-lead:24px}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:var(--color-ground);color:var(--color-ink);font:16px/1.65 var(--font-body)}a{color:inherit}header,footer{padding:24px 5%;border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-line)}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px;margin-top:18px}main>section,article{padding:64px 5%}h1,h2{font-family:var(--font-display);line-height:1.1}h1{font-size:clamp(38px,5vw,64px)}h2{font-size:38px}p{max-width:850px}img{max-width:100%;object-fit:cover}figure{margin:32px 0}figure img{width:100%;max-height:480px}figcaption,small{color:var(--color-muted);font-size:13px}.row{border-top:1px solid var(--color-line);padding:24px 0}input,select,textarea{display:block;width:100%;padding:14px;margin:8px 0 20px;border:1px solid var(--color-muted);font:inherit}button,.button{display:inline-block;padding:14px 24px;background:var(--color-ink);color:var(--color-ground)}:focus-visible{outline:3px solid var(--color-civic);outline-offset:4px}@media(max-width:760px){section{grid-template-columns:1fr!important;padding:32px 5%!important;gap:24px!important}h1{font-size:42px!important}header{display:block!important}main>div{flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px}section>div{max-width:100%}}</style></head><body><a href="#main">Skip to content</a><header><a href="/"><b>PICTURETHIS PRODUCTION</b></a><nav><a href="//">Home</a><a href="/programs/">Programs</a><a href="/faithfire/">Faithfire</a><a href="/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/blog/">Blog</a><a href="/events/">Events</a><a href="/partners/">Partners</a><a href="/get-involved/">Get Involved</a><a href="/donate/">Donate</a></nav></header><main id="main"><section style="padding:64px var(--spacing-page);display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:56px;align-items:center"><div><p style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.13em;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-muted)">A working trade, taught properly · GTA</p><h1 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-hero);font-weight:600;line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:var(--spacing-item) 0">Learn production.<br>Work real shows.<br>Get paid for it.</h1><p style="font-size:var(--text-h3);max-width:530px;color:var(--color-muted)">No experience, no gear, no connections — if you are 16 to 29 and curious, there is a place for you here.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:var(--spacing-item);align-items:center;margin-top:var(--spacing-block)"><a href="/get-involved/" style="background:var(--color-ink);color:var(--color-surface);padding:16px 28px;border-radius:var(--radius-ngo);font-weight:700">Join the next cohort</a><a href="/programs/" style="font-weight:700;border-bottom:1px solid">Explore our programs</a></div></div><figure style="margin:0;position:relative"><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/28643185.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Audio engineer working at a mixing console during an outdoor event" style="width:100%;height:430px;object-fit:cover;border-radius:var(--radius-ngo)"><figcaption style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:8px">Hands on the gear. Part of a real crew. · Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure></section><div style="margin:0 var(--spacing-page);padding:var(--spacing-item) var(--spacing-block);background:var(--color-accent-soft);display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-weight:600"><span>Next cohort: January · Applications open</span><span>Saturdays 10:00 AM–2:00 PM</span><span>Free · Gear provided · Twenty places</span></div><section style="padding:64px var(--spacing-page);display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:88px"><div><p style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.12em">Our purpose</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-h1);line-height:1.12;margin:var(--spacing-tight) 0 var(--spacing-item)">A skill. A crew.<br>A different offer.</h2><p style="color:var(--color-muted)">PictureThis Production trains young people aged 16 to 29 in sound, cameras, lighting and streaming, then puts them on real crews with a working technician as a mentor. A skill, a crew that knows your name, and paid work at the end of it — a different offer than the street makes.</p><p style="margin-top:var(--spacing-item);font-weight:600">We teach the craft and the conduct — both are the job.</p><p style="margin-top:var(--spacing-block)"><span style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:var(--color-muted)">Our vision</span> — A Toronto where young people build their future together, not on the streets, but as a crew: learning skills, earning a living, and looking out for one another.</p><p style="margin-top:var(--spacing-item)"><span style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:var(--color-muted)">Our mission</span> — PictureThis Production gives young people ages 16 to 29 free, hands-on training and mentorship in live production, in sound, cameras, lighting, and streaming, on real shows alongside working technicians. They leave with a trade, a reference, and a path into paid work.</p></div><div><div class="row"><small>01</small><h3>Saturday training</h3><p>Four hours, hands on the gear. Consoles, cameras, cable and comms — the same equipment the Sunday crew uses, not classroom props.</p></div><div class="row"><small>02</small><h3>Sunday and event crews</h3><p>From week three you are on a real crew at Faithfire Production or a partner venue: line check, rehearsal, service, pack-down. Supervised, but real.</p></div><div class="row"><small>03</small><h3>A mentor who works in the industry</h3><p>Every participant is paired with a working technician. Monthly check-ins, honest feedback, and help landing your first freelance bookings.</p></div></div></section><section aria-label="Production equipment in focus" style="padding:0 var(--spacing-page) var(--spacing-block);display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:var(--spacing-item)"><div class="row"><small>01</small><h3>Saturday training</h3><p>Hands on real gear, with a working crew.</p></div><div class="row"><small>01</small><h3>Saturday training</h3><p>Hands on real gear, with a working crew.</p></div></section><section id="programs" style="padding:64px var(--spacing-page);background:var(--color-surface)"><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:end;margin-bottom:var(--spacing-block)"><div><p style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.12em;color:var(--color-muted)">PROGRAMS / TRAINING · MENTORING · PATHWAYS · PRODUCTION MANAGEMENT · TECHNICAL MANAGEMENT</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-h1);line-height:1.15;margin-top:var(--spacing-tight)">Eight weeks. Real skills.</h2></div><p style="max-width:350px;color:var(--color-muted)">Everyone starts at week 1 together.<br>Nobody is behind.</p></div><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:56px"><div><div class="row"><small>WK 01</small><h3>Cables, connectors and signal flow</h3><p>Coil, tape and test every cable type we own. Follow a signal from microphone to speaker and find the break we hid in it.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 02</small><h3>Digital consoles: the Wing and X32</h3><p>Build a show file from empty: inputs, gains, EQ, routing, scenes. The two consoles most GTA rooms actually run.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 03</small><h3>Mixing a live band</h3><p>Soundcheck a real band at Faithfire. Vocals first, then everything else. Learn what to do when it feeds back anyway.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 04</small><h3>Cameras and video switching (ATEM)</h3><p>Frame, focus and follow. Then sit in the director’s chair and call a two-camera rehearsal on comms.</p></div></div><div><div class="row"><small>WK 05</small><h3>Lighting: rigging safely, running a desk</h3><p>Hang, focus and cable a rig without hurting anyone. Program cues and run a service from the lighting desk.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 06</small><h3>Streaming, lyrics and media</h3><p>Build a full ProPresenter service file: lyrics, scripture, sermon slides, stream output. Advance it live, on the director’s call.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 07</small><h3>Show calling, comms and event etiquette</h3><p>Call a show start to finish. Comms discipline, dress codes, client manners — the conduct half of the job.</p></div><div class="row"><small>WK 08</small><h3>Capstone: crew a real production</h3><p>A full event, crewed entirely by the cohort, mentors on comms only if needed. This is the reference you graduate with.</p></div></div></div><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:56px;margin-top:var(--spacing-item);padding-top:var(--spacing-item);border-top:1px solid var(--color-line)"><p><b>Mentoring that takes care.</b> All mentors are screened with a Vulnerable Sector Check, work to a written code of conduct, and meet participants in crew spaces, never alone off-site.</p><p><b>Pathways, not promises.</b> We don’t promise jobs. We promise hours on real gear, a reference from people the industry knows, and a crew that will say your name when a call comes in.</p><p><b>Run the whole show.</b> Production Management. The other half of putting on a show: advancing the event, building the run sheet and call sheets, managing the budget, and calling the show on the day. Participants who work well on a crew learn to run the production, not just handle one role in it.</p><p><b>Lead the department.</b> Technical Management. For those who go on to lead a department: planning the rig, choosing and maintaining the gear, signing off on safety, and teaching the next cohort. It's the top of the pathway, the people who spec out the room and are accountable for it.</p></div><div style="margin-top:var(--spacing-block);padding-top:var(--spacing-item);border-top:1px solid var(--color-line)"><p style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:var(--color-muted)">Our standards</p><ul style="margin:var(--spacing-tight) 0 0;padding-left:20px;color:var(--color-muted)"><li>Every mentor passes a Vulnerable Sector Check before working with participants and follows a written code of conduct.</li><li>Mentors and participants meet in crew spaces, never alone off-site.</li><li>Participants aged 16 and 17 join with a parent or guardian's consent.</li><li>Photos and recordings of participants are used only with consent, which can be withdrawn at any time.</li><li>The program is free, and it stays free. No fees, no gear purchases, no paid upsells.</li></ul></div><figure style="margin:var(--spacing-block) 0 0"><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/8412361.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Two camera operators filming an indoor live event" style="width:100%;height:430px;object-fit:cover;border-radius:var(--radius-ngo)"><figcaption style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:8px">Hands on the gear. Part of a real crew. · Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure></section><section><p>THE PEOPLE BEHIND THE CREW</p><blockquote>“Working on the production team has been one of the most rewarding things I have done. It gave me room to grow in ways I never expected.”</blockquote><p>Veronica Alexander · Crew Chief, FaithFire Production</p><p>Joined the production team, grew into Technical Manager, promoted to Crew Chief at the end of the year.</p></section><section id="involved" style="padding:64px var(--spacing-page)"><p style="font-size:var(--text-small);letter-spacing:.12em;color:var(--color-muted)">GET INVOLVED</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-h1);margin:8px 0 var(--spacing-block)">Three ways to keep the program free.</h2><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:40px"><div class="row"><small>01</small><h3>Mentor</h3><p>Working production credits? Commit two evenings a month, or two Sundays on crew, for eight weeks. A police check is required; we arrange it and cover the cost.</p></div><div id="donate"><div class="row"><small>02</small><h3>Donate</h3><p>$40 buys course materials. $90 covers a month of TTC fares. $250 supports the shared gear library. Working gear donations are welcome across the GTA.</p></div></div><div class="row"><small>03</small><h3>Partner</h3><p>Host a training Saturday or take supervised trainees onto your event crew. A room, a Saturday, a working rig — and first call on graduates when you are hiring.</p></div></div><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:40px;margin-top:var(--spacing-block);padding-top:var(--spacing-item);border-top:1px solid var(--color-line);align-items:start"><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:20px"><li>$40 covers one participant's course materials for the full eight weeks.</li><li>$90 covers a term of transit stipends so a participant can actually get to training.</li><li>$250 goes to the gear library: the shared kit participants learn on and borrow for first calls.</li></ul><figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/4330788.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Cables and connectors on a shelf in the gear library" style="width:100%;height:280px;object-fit:cover;border-radius:var(--radius-ngo)"><figcaption style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:8px">Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure></div></section><section><h2>Come as you are.</h2><a class="button" href="/get-involved/">Get involved</a></section><section><h2>The first cohort wasn't a class. It was a crew.</h2><p>Faithfire Production is where PictureThis got its start: a church production team at 139 Millwick Dr that grew into a full production department, one show at a time. Most of the crew started with little to no experience. They learned by shadowing someone more experienced, helping out on real shows, and then running their own positions.</p><p>These days, that crew handles two Sunday services plus events like Cue &amp; A and Revival Hub, and other churches and production teams specifically request them. They are the mentor bench for the January cohort, cohort two. The eight-week program formalizes what they lived informally.</p><a href="/faithfire/">Meet Cohort One</a></section><section><h2>News from the crew</h2><p>Stories will appear after editorial approval.</p><a href="/blog/">News & articles</a></section></main><footer><p>Training: Faithfire Production, 139 Millwick Dr, Toronto.</p><p>PictureThis Production is being set up as a nonprofit organization in Ontario.</p><p>Faithfire Production is its production arm.</p><a href="/policies/">Our standards & policies</a> · <a href={"mailto:"+contactEmail}>{contactEmail}</a> · Placeholder contact</footer></body></html>